The latest glyphs bypassing browser safety checks allow tricksters to run websites from a clearly fake domain name (when displayed in Punycode), although they appear just like the real deal in Unicode.The characters of note, in this case, are ө, which is found in various Cyrillic languages such as Kazakh, Mongolian, and Tatar, and the Latin K with hook, ƙ, used in Hausa and Karai-karai.Both are visually similar to the letters e/o, i, and k, respectively, and allowed the researchers to register 20 lookalike domain names.These included: aррӏө[.]comsрасөх[.]comoƙta[.]comniƙe[.]comBelow are the URLs’ Punycode equivalents – how browsers should display them safely:xn--80a6aa68c8d.comxn--80a5aeq0fr0c.comxn--ota-f6a.comxn--nie-g6a.comYou can try them out; they’re registered by Have I Been Squatted and are safe to visit.

