They take you to research demo pages set up by the researchers, and each page explains how exactly they bypass browser protections.Crucially, they all bypass the key security measures deployed by Chromium-based browsers.How the bypasses workBrowsers deploy two main defense layers. The first is a set of seven sequential checks run by Chromium’s SafeToDisplayAsUnicode function, which check for a range of common spoofing methods. Vendors began introducing these checks in 2017 after researcher Xudong Zheng registered аррӏе.com – a domain consisting of all-Cyrillic characters.Chromium’s checks, which factor in a hardcoded list of known Cyrillic characters known to be used in place of Latin letters, can be seen as "all or nothing."Built to detect all-Cyrillic strings, the measure only takes action against domain names if every character in the string is on its hardcoded list.  If one character is missing, the check is bypassed. Characters such as ө, ї, and ү, are known as “breakers,” as these are not present in the lookalike list, as of Chrome 154  (released to stable channel on September 22).

